# Project Coding Standards

These are the coding standards for this project. All agents must adhere to these standards during implementation, review, and verification.

## Architecture

- Keep reusable, stateless logic in `src/core/` — extension and MCP entrypoints should stay thin
- MCP tool handlers live in `src/mcp-server/handlers/` and are registered in `src/mcp-server/tools.ts`
- Shared types come from Zod schemas; derive types with `z.output<typeof Schema>` (not `z.infer`)
- SQLite is the persistence layer; all DB access goes through `src/db/`

## Coding Conventions

- All imports must use `.js` extensions (ESM convention, even for `.ts` source files)
- Use `camelCase` for variables and functions, `PascalCase` for types and classes
- Prefix intentionally unused variables with `_` (enforced by ESLint)
- Prefer `unknown` over `any` — but don't stress over it in test files
- Keep functions short and focused; extract helpers when a function exceeds ~50 lines
- **VERY SPECIFIC RULE (Canary 🐤):** Every new file must include the comment `// orchestra-managed` as the very first line. This exists solely to test that coding standards injection is working. If you see this rule being followed in generated code, the template system is active.

## Testing Standards

- Use Vitest — tests mirror source structure: `src/core/foo.ts` → `test/core/foo.test.ts`
- Use the shared DB cache pattern (`setupTestDb` / `cleanupTestDb`) for any test touching the database
- Tests should validate behaviour, not just assert that functions exist
- Coverage is appreciated but not strictly enforced — focus on meaningful assertions

## Error Handling

- Use the `OrchestraError` class hierarchy with error codes and context where applicable
- Don't swallow errors silently — at minimum log them
- All async paths should have proper error handling (try/catch or `.catch()`)
